# Metal-Organic Frameworks as a Versatile Platform for Proton Conductors

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are an intriguing type of crystalline porous materials that can be readily built from metal ions or clusters and organic linkers. Recently, MOF materials, featuring high surface areas, rich structural tunability, and functional pore surfaces, which can accommodate a variety of guest molecules as proton carriers and to systemically regulate the proton concentration and mobility within the available space, have attracted tremendous attention for their roles as solid electrolytes in fuel cells. Recent advances in MOFs as a versatile platform for proton conduction in the field of humidity condition proton-conduction, anhydrous atmosphere proton-conduction, single-crystal proton-conduction, and including MOF-based membranes for fuel cells, are summarized and highlighted. Furthermore, the challenges, future trends, and prospects of MOF materials for solid electrolytes are also discussed.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are an intriguing type of crystalline porous materials that can be readily built from metal ions or clusters and organic linkers. Recently, MOF materials, featuring high surface areas, rich structural tunability, and functional pore surfaces, which can accommodate a variety of guest molecules as proton carriers and to systemically regulate the proton concentration and mobility within the available space, have attracted tremendous attention for their roles as solid electrolytes in fuel cells. Recent advances in MOFs as a versatile platform for proton conduction in the field of humidity condition proton-conduction, anhydrous atmosphere protonconduction, single-crystal proton-conduction, and including MOF-based membranes for fuel cells, are summarized and highlighted. Furthermore, the challenges, future trends, and prospects of MOF materials for solid electrolytes are also discussed.
## 1. Introduction
World population is estimated to increase from current 7.7 billion people up to 9.7 billion around 2050.[1] In order to cope with the energy demands of the fast urbanization and population growth, we must double the present energy production of 14 TW $1\ \mathrm{TW}=10^{12}$ W) by 2050.[2] Nowadays, the widely used energy resources mainly derives from fossil fuels, such as coal, oil and natural gas, accounting for $80\%$ of the world’s total energy supply.[3] The combustion of fossil fuels will release various toxic gases and volatile organic compounds, involving a series of environmental issues, especially for the global climate warming owing to greenhouse effect. The global $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions from the use of fossil fuels were estimated in $37.4\mathrm{Gt}$ $(1~{\mathrm{Gt}}=10^{9}~\mathrm{t})$ in 2018,[4] which is one of the chief perpetrators of the greenhouse effect.[5] Therefore, it is an urgent demand to develop alternative cleaner energy resources to alleviate environmental pressures and reduce the sole relying on fossil fuels.
In recent few decades, several renewable energy sources, such as solar energy, wind, geothermal heat, tides and biomass, have been developed, but these energies are all inherently intermittent and usually dispersed.[6] Except for these, the fuel cell (FC) technology is an attractive energy conversion system for energy production with clean energy sources, such as hydrogen $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ , methanol $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})$ , and ethanol $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH})$ .[7,8] The considerable efforts have been invested in the development of this field resulting in several different types of fuel cell technologies, mainly including: i) proton exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs),[9] ii) phosphoric acid fuel cells (PAFCs),[10] iii) solid oxide fuel cells (SOFCs),[11] and iv) alkaline fuel cells (AFC).[12,13] Among them, PEMFC is recognized as a promising candidates for replacing the traditional engines owing to their ultralow emission, high power density, and quick start-up features. In a typical $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ fuel cell device, the electrons (e−) are drawn from the fuels $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ to produce protons $\left(\mathrm{H^{+}}\right)$ in anode. Then, the electrons passed through an external circuit, transferred to the cathode and thus generated current, at the same time, the protons transported across the proton exchange membrane (PEM) to the cathode, converting the chemical energy into electrical energy. The product of this chemical reaction is only water; thus, this converting system has been regarded as a clean and sustainable technology.
Recently, the Energy Efficiency and Renewable Energy Office of the US Department of Energy (DOE) has reset new targets to guide the development of fuel cell technology.[14] Proton exchange membranes seem to be at the heart of fuel cell technology, so the design and synthesis of new solid electrolyte materials is critical to the efficient operation and achievement of these ambitious targets. The promising candidates of PEMs should meet the following requirements: a) high proton conductivity $(\sigma>10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1})$ , b) good chemical and thermal stability under fuel cell operating conditions, c) superior gas barrier properties, d) good membrane (thin-film) mechanical strength and processability, e) good compatibility with other components (bipolar plates, electrode materials, etc.), and f) easy large-scale production with low cost.
The first proton conductive material utilized in practical fuel cell applications was Nafion, a perfluorinated sulfonated polymer, discovered by Walther T. Grot from DuPont in the late 1960s.[15] However, the conductivity value of Nafion will suffer from significant damage when temperatures are heated up to $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ or freezed below $-5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , moreover, it will also hinder their large-scale use owing to the high costs and complex synthesis process.[16] On the other hand, due to the amorphous nature, it is hard to investigate the proton conduction behavior and regulate the proton transport pathway. A large number of new proton conductive materials, such as solid acids,[17,18] ceramic oxides,[19] heterocyclic organic molecules,[20] and organic polymers,[21] were discovered through the persistent research during the 1970s and 1980s. Generally, solid acids and ceramic oxides operate at intermediate temperature $(120-300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and high temperature $(600{-}1000\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ range, respectively, with the maximum conductivity value up to $10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ . In addition to above these materials, the Keggin type heteropoly acid (HPA, e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40})$ with crystalline properties is another new type of proton conductor material, due to their unique structural features of fast charge-transfer and rich proton acceptors, which was first explored by Nakamurar in 1979.[22] However, similar to commercial Nafion, the proton conductive performances of such material is significantly dropped at the temperatures above $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) represent an intriguing type of crystalline porous materials that can be readily selfassembled through the coordination of metal ions/clusters with organic linkers.[23–26] Recently, MOFs have received widespread attention for their role as solid electrolytes (also known as proton conductors or proton exchange membranes) in fuel cells. The high surface areas, rich structural tunability, and functional pore surface of MOFs provide great opportunities to load a variety of guest molecules as proton carriers and to systemically modify the proton concentration and mobility within the available spaces (Scheme  1).[27–33] In addition, the crystalline nature of MOFs endows an opportunity to investigate the proton transport pathway and mechanism, which will provide theoretical guidance for the design and synthesis of novel proton-conducting materials. Until now, generally including four types (types I to IV) of strategies have been proposed to regulate the proton conductivity of crystalline porous materials.[34,35] As shown in Scheme 1, the simplest one (type I) is to generate proton carriers as counter ions, such as $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ , and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ , into the pores of MOFs.[36–38] In type II, incorporating acidic functional groups (e.g., $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , − $\mathrm{PO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , −COOH, and $-\mathrm{OH}$ moieties) into the host framework to improve the proton concentration and mobility within the pore.[39–42] In type III, loading charge-neutral proton carries (e.g., nonvolatile acids, ionic liquids, organic carboxyl, hydroxyl, or heterocyclic molecules) into the voids of MOFs by post-synthesis modification.[43–47] In the last one (type IV), some unique avenues could also significantly affect the mobility or concentration of proton carriers, such as structure phase transformation, metal/ligand substitution, and ordered structural defects.[48–50] In addition, covalent organic frameworks (COFs)[51–53] and hydrogen-bonded organic frameworks (HOFs)[54–57] have also emerged as a new type of crystalline material for proton conductors. Particularly, COFs possess some unique features: relatively lightweight, various functionalities, high chemical, thermal and mechanical stability, which makes it attractive as new type of proton conductors.[58] Several thematic review papers have shown the rapid developments of crystalline porous materials (CPMs, mainly including MOFs, COFs, and HOFs) as proton conductors before 2016.[34,59–70] Herein, we
