## 2. MOFs as a Versatile Platform for Proton Conductors
## 2.1. Proton Conduction under Humidity Condition
The first example of coordination polymer (CP)/metal–organic framework based proton conductive material was reported by Kanda et  al. in 1979,[71] with a series of 2D $\mathbf{\Psi}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -disubstituted dithiooxamido (dtoa) $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ complexes, although the conductive mechanism was unclear due to its amorphous nature. Until 2003, Kitagawa et  al. realized an isomorphic hydrogendoped $(\mathrm{HOC_{2}H_{4}})_{2}\mathrm{dtoa-Cu}$ sample,[72] with the $\sigma$ value of $2.2\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $27~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $100\%$ relative humidity (RH). Although the conductivity value in series type of coordination polymer is not very high, it opens a novel direction for the study of CP/MOF-based proton conductors (Scheme 2).
In particular, when MOF materials are explored as solid electrolytes, their structure integrity must be guaranteed to keep their inherent functionalities and features under working condition. Therefore, the water stability is always a prerequisite to investigate the proton conductive behaviors for MOF-based conductors under humid conditions. Here, we mainly focus on the summary of some representative works by employing stable
MOFs as platform to prepare proton conductors and review their performances under humidity condition.
## 2.1.1. Oxalate-Based MOFs
Oxalate-based MOF is a typical class of proton conductors that have been extensively studied.[73–77] Among these, ferrous oxalate dihydrate $\mathrm{[Fe(ox)}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{]}^{\left[73\right]}$ is worth mentioning, which was constructed by the Fe(II) ions, oxalate ions and coordinated water molecules. At $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH, $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{ox})\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ exhibited a high proton conductivity, with the $\sigma$ value of $1.3\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ . It is worth noting that $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{ox})\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is a fast-ionic conductor material because of its relatively high conductivity and low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value of $0.37\ \mathrm{eV}.$ In such structure, the well-ordered coordinated water molecule can be regarded not only as proton source, but also as a proton transfer pathway.
Subsequently, the proton conduction behavior of another type of oxalate-based framework, $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ [36] was also explored by Kitagawa group in 2009. In this compound, the oxalate anions bridged the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ ions and generated a typical 2D honeycomb layers of $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{ox}_{)3}]^{2-}$ , in which the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ acts as counterion to balance the framework charge. In addition, the hexagonal pore cavities were filled with the adipic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp}\right)$ and water molecules (Figure  1a). Within the pore spaces, the 2D hydrogen-bonding networks can be generated from the adipic acids, water molecules, ammonium ions, and oxalate anions from the host framework (Figure 1c). This oxalate-based coordination polymer achieved a superprotonic conductivity $(8\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ , $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH), with the activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ value of $0.63\ \mathrm{eV},$ and indicated the proton transport through the self-diffusion vehicle mechanism ( $\mathrm{\mathit{E}_{a}}>0.5\:\mathrm{eV})$ .[78] This is the first example having realized ${\approx}10^{-2}~\mathrm{S}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ superproton conductivity among the MOF-based proton conductors under room temperature, which demonstrated that the introduction of counter cations or acid molecules as proton sources/carriers was an effective strategy for increasing the proton conductivity. To further explore the role of hydrogen-bonding networks in proton conductive properties, Kitagawa and co-workers carried out the following two aspects of research, one of them was to tune the water content within the pores (Figure  1d),[79] and the other was to substitute the counter cations (Figure  1e).[80] It can be clearly observed that the proton conduction pathways in the pores can be finely tuned by water vapor adsorption/ desorption process, and the conductivity value range from $\approx10^{-12}\ \mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ (anhydrate phases) to ${\approx}10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (trihydrate phases). After that, they constructed a novel isomorphic MOF, $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},^{[80]}$ where the $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ ions were substituted by potassium $(\mathrm{K^{+}})$ ions, while maintaining the original 2D layered framework. Under $98\%$ RH and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ condition, this new MOF exhibited a $\sigma$ value of $1.2\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , which was about two orders of magnitude lower than original $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ ion loading sample. The key factor is that substituted potassium ion in the pore cannot form the well-defined hydrogenbonding network with other proton sources. Although these oxalate-bridged crystalline MOFs exhibit excellent proton conductive performances, their water stability, cycling, and durability have not been fully studied.
## 2.1.2. Phosphonate-Based MOFs
The metal phosphonate frameworks are a typical organic– inorganic hybrid material,[81] which have gain widespread attention for their application in many research areas such as ion exchange,[82] intercalation chemistry,[83] proton conduction,[66] and catalysis,[84] attributed to the rich coordination modes of the phosphonate groups and exceptional chemical and thermal stability of these materials.[85] In fact, the metal phosphonates first time explored in proton conduction can be dated back to1990s.[86–88] Alberti et al. reported a series of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -phosphonate frameworks (e.g., $\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{RPO}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{ZP(Y)}_{0.46}$ , and ZrPhMeOH), and the good proton conductivities were found in several candidates with the $\sigma$ value up to $10^{-2}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ,[88] however, the proton transfer mechanism in such material was not clearly investigated owing to the lack of single crystal structures. Nowadays, with the rapid development of X-ray diffraction technology, quite a larger number of crystal structures of metal phosphonate frameworks have been acquired. Thus, it promotes extensive research on metal phosphate frameworks in proton conducting materials.[66,89–92]
## 2.1.3. MIL-101-Based MOFs
In recent years, the highly charged metal-containing secondary building units (SBUs) were frequently employed to fabricate chemically stable MOFs (e.g., Zr-MOFs and MIL series of MOFs).[98,99] Since the pioneer Férey et  al. first reported the Cr-based MOF (named MIL-101) in 2005,[100] it has been extensively studied in many fields, such as gas adsorption and separation,[101] heterogeneous catalytic,[102] and proton conduction,[43,44,103–106] which is mainly attributed to its high surface area, mesoporous feature and excellent chemical stability.
In 2012, Fedin and co-workers investigated the proton conductive performances of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}$ - 101,[43] it was prepared by simply impregnating nonvolatile ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ into the porous structure of MIL-101. At $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and low humidity $(0.13\%$ RH), the proton conductivity reached up to $1\times10^{-2}$ and $3\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ , respectively, for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ , which was superior to most MOF-based proton conductors and comparable to commercial Nafion. This contribution provided a convenient strategy to realize high proton conductivity by loading the acidic moiety into the porous structure of MOFs.
Based on the similar strategy, Lan and co-workers[105] employed sulfonated MIL-101 $(\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})$ as host framework and then impregnated different concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ (1, 2, and $3\mathrm{~M~}$ ) into the mesoporous of MOF in 2017 (Figure 3a). As expected, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $\left(3\mathrm{~M~}\right)$ exhibited the ultrahigh proton conductivity of $1.82\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ under $90\%$ RH and $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which surpassed other representative MOF-based proton conductors under similar conditions. In particular, under the subzero temperature $(-40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , the proton conductivity of $0.92\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ (3 m) realized a significant improvement by comparison with other MOF-based proton conductors (Figure  3b,c). The extraordinary proton conduction performances of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $(3\mathrm{~~\boldmath~{~M~}~})$ were mainly owing to the synergistic effect of multiple effective proton units.
In order to investigate the effect of the proton-conducting pathway on electrolyte performance, Liu and co-workers[106] reported two MIL-101-based MOFs separately containing zigzag and linear proton conduction pathways by impregnation or immobilization of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ (HPW) molecules into the different nanocages, and demonstrated that the linear pathway is more favorable for proton transport than the zigzag one. Moreover, the proton conductivity can be further increased to $1.52\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ , at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $100\%$ RH, by loading flexible polyamine molecules into the HPW-impregnated MIL-101 samples (Figure 3d–f).
Nowadays, most of electrolyte materials typically have a significant decrease of proton conductivity at low relative humidity. Hence, it is highly desirable to develop smart proton conductors that can preserve proton transport pathway over a wide RH range. Recently, Chen and co-workers[30] reported a flexible MOF (BUT-8(Cr)) with excellent chemical stability, which was constructed from sulfonic acid $(\mathrm{-}\mathrm{{SO}_{3}H})$ functionalized naphthalene-2,6-dicarboxylate organic linker and $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6}$ SBUs. After treatment with 0.5 m ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ aqueous solution, the counter cations $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}\mathrm{)}$ in BUT-8(Cr) can fully exchange with protons to yield BUT- $\mathrm{\Omega^{3}(C r)A}$ (Figure  4a,b). As expected, BUT-8 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle\mathrm{A}$ displayed excellent proton conductivity over a wide relative humidity and reached a maximum $\sigma$ value of $1.27\times$ $10^{-1}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ( $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $100\%$ RH, Figure 4c,d). The unique proton conductive behavior was observed in BUT- $3(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{A}$ not only due to the presence of a large amount of hydrophilic functionalized $-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ sites on the pore surface, but also attributed to the flexible framework exhibited a moisture-dependent structural transformation, so-called framework “self-adaption” capability.
## 2.1.4. Zr-Based MOFs
Since Lillerud and co-workers first time reported the UiO-66 series $\mathrm{zr}$ -MOF in 2008,[107,108] it has been extensively investigated in many fields, for example gas storage,[109] separation,[110] and heterogeneous catalytic,[111,112] attributed to their high porosity, exceptional chemical and thermal stability. The prototype UiO-66 was constructed from $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ SBUs and terephtalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}B D C)$ linker, featuring triangular pores of $6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . Considering that the aromatic ring of the organic linker is susceptible to be functionalized, thus UiO-66 may be a suitable candidate to explore the relationship between structure and proton conductive performance. In 2015, Hong and coworkers[40] adopted the facile post-synthetic oxidation strategy to obtain the sulfonated UiO-66, UiO-66 $(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})_{2}$ the free $-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ groups covalently linked to the host framework (Figure  5a). UiO-66 $\mathrm{\Gamma}(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})_{2}$ exhibited a superprotonic conductivity of
$8.4\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $90\%$ RH (Figure 5b), and preserved excellent proton conductivity for a long-term (over $96\mathrm{~h~}$ ) under this condition. Subsequently, Li and co-workers[113,114] reported a series of isostructural UiO-66-X $(\mathrm{X}~=~\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , 2COOH, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{~H~}$ , or Br) to explore the relationship between structure and proton-conducting properties. The proton conductivity was up to $0.34\times10^{-2}$ and $0.10\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ for UiO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and UiO-66-2COOH, respectively, at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $97\%$ RH, which was attributed to the host framework with highly acidic and strong hydrophilic functional groups. To understand the effect of crystal defects on the physical properties of solid materials, Kitagawa and co-workers[50] chose UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ as an ideal candidate to investigate the relationship between ordered defect sublattice and proton conducting properties. Theoretical calculations indicated that the charge trapping sites of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ cluster seemed to be more saturated after acid treatment of the MOF. Especially for the presence of sulfoacetic acid, the proton conductivity showed a fivefold improvement and reached the maximum $\sigma$ value of $5.62\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH.
Nowadays, most of MOF-based proton conductive materials still remain some drawbacks, such as cumbersome ligand preparation, difficulty in scale production, and using toxic chemicals. Lately, Serre and co-workers realized a new $\mathrm{zr}$ -based MOF MIP-202(Zr) (Figure  5c),[115] constructed from commercialized aspartic acid and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ SBUs, isostructural with the well-study MOF-801.[116] It was worth mentioning that MIP202 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ exhibited green, cost-effective and scalable preparation characteristics, and with the space-time production of more than $7000\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kg}~}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}^{-3}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\day}}^{-1}$ . In addition, $\mathrm{MIP-}202(\mathrm{Zr})$ featured superior proton conduction properties with the $\sigma$ value of $1.1\times$ $10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH (Figure  5d), and even can work over 7 days under this conditions.
## 2.1.5. HKUST-1-Based MOFs
The well-known MOF, Cu-BTC (also known as HKUST-1, MOF-199), was first reported by Williams and co-workers in 1999,[117] constructed from $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ paddlewheel units and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate (BTC) linkers, and possessed three different types of nanocages with the diameter of $\approx4,10$ , and $11\textup{\AA}$ , respectively. In 2012, Farha and co-workers[118] employed HKUST-1 as a platform to modulate the ionic conductivity by post-synthesis modification coordination solvent (including $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , MeOH, EtOH, and MeCN) at the open Cu sites. At ambient temperature and methanol atmosphere, the conductivity of HKUST-1 can be increased by ${\approx}75$ -fold, when the terminal coordinated MeCN molecules were replaced by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. The water stability of pristine HKUST-1 limited the further modification and application, but, when the immobilization of polyoxometalate (POM) anions into the nanocage of
HKUST-1 (named as NENU-3, isomorphism with HKUST-1) can remarkably improve the hydrothermal and chemical stability.[119,120]
Since Kitagawa pioneered the study of loading heterocyclic organic molecules (e.g., imidazole or benzimidazole)[47,121–127] as proton carriers into the channels of MOFs to promote proton transport, it has become an effective approach to develop high-performance electrolytes. However, it is still ambiguous that the imidazole molecules in bound or free states are more favorable for proton conduction. In order to cope with such confusion, Chen and co-workers chose the highly stable MOF (NENU-3) as platform,[29] and employed the one- and two-step obtain two imidazole incorporating proton conductors, namely as $\mathrm{Im}@(\mathrm{NENU}{-3})$ and $\operatorname{Im-Cu}({a})$ (NENU-3a), respectively (Figure  6a). To make our big surprise was that the proton conductivity of $\mathrm{Im}@(\mathrm{NENU}{\cdot}3)$ $(1.82\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ was ${\approx}58$ times higher than that of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Im-Cu}\ @(\mathrm{NENU}\cdot3\mathrm{a})$ $(3.16\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ at $90\%$ RH and $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 6b,d). Notably, single crystal structure revealed that the terminal bound imidazole in $\mathrm{Im\mathrm{-}C u}@(\mathrm{NENU-}3\mathrm{a})$ isolated guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules and then blocked proton transfer pathway. In contrast, the high concentration of free imidazole molecules within $\mathrm{Im}@(\mathrm{NENU}{-3})$ ) can form a continuous hydrogen bond network, which can remarkably facilitate proton-transport (Figure 6c,e). Interestingly, the experimental phenomena of the above samples are exactly the opposite of the Fe-based MOFs (Im-Fe-MOF and $\operatorname{Im}({\widehat{\underline{{a}}}})$ Fe-MOF) reported by Lan and co-workers[128] In Im-Fe-MOF, the continues proton transport pathway can be achieved by the terminal bound imidazole and the absorbed water molecules under humidity atmosphere. These two works indicated that the similar or identical strategy for improving proton conductivity may not be applicable to different MOFs due to structural diversity, which further again confirms the view that the structure of a MOF determines its function.
## 2.1.6. $\beta$ -PCMOF2-Based MOFs
Proton-conducting metal–organic framework, $\beta$ -PCMOF2, was first reported by Shimizu and co-workers in 2009,[129] a trisodium 2,4,6-trihydroxy-1,3,5-trisulfonate benzene $(\mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{L}1)$ coordination polymer, containing 1D channel decorated with sulfonate oxygen atoms. By employing the concept of isomorphous replacement in which the trisulfonate ligand on the framework was partially substituted by tris(hydrogen phosphonate) ligand, obtained a new isostructural material $\mathrm{PCMOF}2^{1}/_{2}$ (Figure  7a).[49] The proton conductivity $(2.1\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ , at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $90\%$ RH) of $\mathrm{PCMOF}2^{1}/_{2}$ was increased by 1.5 orders of magnitude compared to the pristine $\beta{\mathrm{-PCMOF}}2$ $(1.3\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ under similar condition, since the pores would be partially arranged with hydrogen phosphonate groups. In 2009,[129] Shimizu and co-workers achieved a significant increase in anhydrous proton conductivity of $\beta{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{PCMOF}2$ by loading a small amount of amphiprotic heterocyclic guest molecules (1H-1,2,4-triazole) into the pores (Figure 7b). Based on the previous study, in the recent work, Shimizu and co-workers[130] adopted the synergistic effect design strategies (amphiprotic molecule doping and isomorphous ligand substitution simultaneously) to enhance the proton conduction performances of $\beta$ -PCMOF2. As expected, the proton conductivity of the resulting new material, $\mathrm{PCMOF}2^{1}/{}_{2}(\mathrm{Pz})$ and $\mathrm{PCMOF}2^{1}/_{2}(\mathrm{Tz})$ $\mathrm{\DeltaPz}=1\mathrm{H}$ -pyrazole, $\mathrm{Tz}=1\mathrm{H}{\cdot}1,2,4{\cdot}$ triazole), are over $10^{-1}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $90\%$ RH, which was nearly 100-times higher than that of $\beta\mathrm{.}$ -PCMOF2 under identical conditions (Figure 7c,d).
## 2.1.7. Dimethylamine-Based MOFs
It is well-known that the $\mathbf{\Psi}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) solvent is often thermally decomposed to produce dimethylamine $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}\mathrm{)}$ cations during the synthesis of MOF.[131,132] $\ln{2012}$
Banerjee and co-workers reported the first study of that dimethylamine cation not only acted as counter cation to balance the framework charge, but also served as a carrier to promote proton transfer.[133] In this study, they prepared two isostructural metal-organic nanotubes (MONT, termed as In-5TIA and Cd-5TIA), in which the $\ln^{3+}$ (or $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ) ion was coordinated with eight O atoms from four distinct $-\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{-}$ moieties and each carboxylate group chelated to one metal center. Therefore, this two samples afforded the chemical formula of $\mathrm{In}(5{\cdot}\mathrm{TIA})_{2}(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ and $\mathrm{Cd(5\cdotTIA)}_{2}(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2},$ respectively (Figure  8a,b). The proton conductivity was measured of $5.35\times10^{-5}$ and $3.61\ \times\$ $10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ for In-5TIA and Cd-5TIA, respectively, under $98\%$ RH and 301 K. Notably, the conductivity value of Cd-5TIA was almost 70 times high than that of In-5TIA under identical conditions, which was attributed to per SBU in Cd-5TIA possessed two $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ cations, while each SBU in In-5TIA only contained one $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ cation. After that, the same group further reported two isomorphic 2D $\mathrm{{In}(I I I)}$ -based MOFs (In-IA-2D-1 and In-IA-2D-2),[134] with the formula of $\mathrm{\dot{In}(I A)_{2}(M e_{2}N H_{2})(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{In(IA)_{2}(M e_{2}N H_{2})(D M F)}$ , respectively. The conductivity values were found of $3.4\times10^{-3}$ and $4.2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ for InIA-2D-1 and In-IA-2D-2, respectively, under $98\%$ RH and room temperature $(27~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . In addition, In-IA-2D-2 exhibited the typical anhydrous proton conductive behaviors from room temperature to high temperatures $(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , accompanied with the conductivity value of $2.6\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , $2.7\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(77~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , and $1.18\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , respectively, under anhydrous conditions.
Later, Yaghi’s group reported three novel metal catecholates framework (M-CATs),[135] which was constructed from various metal salt $(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+/3+}$ , $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ , and $\mathrm{V}^{4+}$ ) and hexatopic catecholate linker, $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathrm{THO^{6-}}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ triphenylene-2,3,6,7,10,11-hexakis(olate)), termed as Fe-CAT-5, Ti-CAT-5, and V-CAT-5, respectively. The single crystal structure of Fe-CAT-5 showed that each linker was coordinated to three equivalent octahedral $\mathrm{Fe(II/III)}$ ions to form a twofold interpenetrated 3D framework with srs topology. Interestingly, these two interpenetrated host skeletons were found to be connected by an $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ cluster (Figure  9a). By combining various characterization techniques, the plausible chemical formula of Fe-CAT-5, Ti-CAT-5, and V-CAT-5 can be determined as $\mathrm{Fe(THO)}\cdot\mathrm{Fe(SO_{4})(M e_{2}N H_{2})_{3}(S o l v e n t)}_{x},$ Ti(THO) $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{Solvent})_{\gamma}$ and V(THO) $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{Solvent})_{z},$ respectively. Considering the large amount of dimethylamine cations and high-water absorption capacity within the pores, it is indicated that Fe-CAT-5 and Ti-CAT-5 may have good proton conductivity. As expected, Fe-CAT-5 displayed ultrahigh proton conductivity with the value of $5.0\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ under $98\%$ RH and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which almost 2 orders of magnitude higher than that of Ti-CAT-5 $(8.2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ under similar conditions (Figure  9b). The observation implied that the coexistence of $\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ ion-pairs within the pore cavity played a vital role in proton transport.
Recently, Xiang and co-workers reported a novel 3D metallo­ ring-organic framework (MROF-1) for the first time,[28] which possessed charming sextuply interlocked nanocages made up of novel metalloring cluster SBUs, affording the chemical formula of $\mathrm{\Delta[Me_{2}N H_{2}][I n(t h b)(p b d c)]}\cdot x G$ (Figure $^{\mathfrak{g}_{\mathtt{C}}}$ ). Notably, MROF-1 exhibited relative high proton conductivity with the maximum value of $1.72\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $97\%$ RH, higher than the values of most $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{^{+}}$ contained proton conductors under similar conditions (Figure  9d). The activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ of MROF-1 was calculated to be $0.37\ \mathrm{eV}$ based upon the Arrhenius equation, which corresponding to the conventional Grotthus mechanism for proton transfer. According to the single crystal X-ray data, two possible proton transport pathways can be found, as follow: 1) $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ from $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ transported within the metalloring cluster via the integral hydrogen bonded network; 2) along the one-dimension channel through the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. This investigation provided a novel avenue for the fabrication of fascinating multifunctional MOFs by employing the new type of SBUs.
## 2.2. Proton Conduction under Anhydrous Atmosphere
The development of anhydrous proton conductors with working temperatures above $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ remains a major challenge to meet the needs of practical applications. In 2009, Kitagawa and co-workers[47] proposed a novel idea of ​encapsulating the amphiphilic heterocyclic organic molecules (such as imidazole or benzimidazole) with high boiling points into the MIL-53 series of MOFs channel to realize hybrid proton-conductive materials. They chose two types of MIL-53, $\mathsf{A l}(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}})$ (1,4-ndc) and $\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}})$ (1,4-bdc), both of them with 1D channel and high thermal stability (over $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , but with distinct pore shapes and surfaces (Figure  10a). The anhydrous conductivity of $\mathsf{A l}(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}})$ (1,4-ndc)⊃Im was found of $2.2\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which exhibited a 220-fold increase compared to $\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}})$ (1,4-bdc)⊃Im $(1.0\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ under the same temperature (Figure  10c). Nanochannels in $\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}})$ (1,4- ndc) have nonpolar pore surfaces, the polar guest molecule does not interact strongly with the host skeleton. In contrast, the polar pore surface was observed in $\mathrm{{Al}}(\mu_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{{OH}})$ (1,4-bdc), the guest imidazole molecule interacts strongly with the hydroxyl group site of the host framework. Therefore, the difference in the interaction between host framework and guest imidazole molecule was the main cause of the difference in proton conductivity. This is the first investigation of MOF-based proton conductors under anhydrous conditions, but the proton conductivity of such materials is moderate. Subsequently, the same group further used the imidazole derivative $({\mathrm{His}}={\mathrm{his}}.$ tamine) as proton carrier to obtain a new hybrid proton-conducting material $\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}})$ (1,4-ndc)⊃His.[124] The histamine molecule contained three proton donor/acceptor sites from the imidazole ring and amine group (Figure 10b). Notably, the maximum proton conductivity in $\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{ndc}){\=}\mathrm{Hi};$ was measured of $1.7\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which was almost two orders of magnitude higher than previous reported $\mathsf{A l}(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-OH})$ (1,4-ndc)⊃Im $(2.2\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The superior proton conductive performances were realized in this new hybrid material, which attributed to the various structural conformation and molecular isotropy of the histamine molecule.
On-board fuel cell technology required solid electrolytes with superior proton conduction performances not only at intermediate temperatures for work, but also at room temperature and even at subzero temperature for startup. In this context, Zhang and co-workers employed porous polyimide (Td-PNDI and Td-PPI) with high thermal and hydrolytic stability as host framework, and simultaneously selected the amphiphilic imidazole (Im) molecule as proton carrier. As expected, the hybrid materials ( $\operatorname{Im}({\widehat{\underline{{a}}}})$ Td-PNDI and $\mathrm{Im}@\mathrm{Td-PPI}$ ) first realized a wide temperature working range from $-40$ to $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure  11a,b).[16] It was observed for the first time that the hybrid materials (Im@Td-PNDI and $\operatorname{Im}(\underline{{\boldsymbol{a}}})\mathrm{Td-PPI}_{\cdot}$ ) have the typical proton conduction behavior at subzero temperatures $(-40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , with the conductivity value of $4.58\times10^{-7}$ and $2.23\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. In addition, the conductivity value of $\operatorname{Im}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{a}}})$ Td-PPI was remarkably higher than that of $\operatorname{Im}({\widehat{\underline{{a}}}})$ Td-PNDI over the entire operating temperature range, attributing to the lower packing density but higher mobility of proton carrier in mesopore of Td-PPI. The activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ values of $\operatorname{Im}({\widehat{\underline{{a}}}})$ Td-PNDI and $\mathrm{Im}@\mathrm{Td-PPI}$ were calculated to be 0.33 and $0.30\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, based on the Arrhenius equation, which indicated that the proton transport through the well-defined Grotthuss mechanism. Although the proton conductive performance and operating temperature range of the above two hybrid materials are general, it will promote further investigation in wide temperature range (especially for subzero-temperature) anhydrous proton conducting materials in the near future.
Recently, the same group expanded the strategy of “acid– base pairs” into the periodic mesoporous organosilicas (PMOs) for the design and preparation of novel anhydrous proton conductors with high proton conductivity in wide temperature window.[136] The resulting $\mathrm{Im}@\mathrm{{s}}{\cdot}\mathrm{PMO}$ displayed superior proton conduction performances over wide temperature range from $-40$ to $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in anhydrous conditions (Figure  12b). Notably, its proton conductivity of $7.11\times10^{-3}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ was higher than the values of representative imidazole-loaded proton conductors, such as TPB-DMTP-COF $(4.37\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1};$ $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})^{[53]}$ and $\mathrm{[Zn(HPO_{4})(H_{2}P O_{4})_{2}](I m H_{2})}$ $(2.6\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ .[122] More importantly, $\mathrm{Im}@\mathrm{{s}}{\cdot}\mathrm{PMO}$ exhibited the widest temperature operating range reported today, and thus set a new benchmark for wide-temperature-range proton conductive materials (Figure  12c). This study will encourage the development of periodic-mesoporous-organosilica as a new type of solid electrolyte for wide temperature range fuel cell application.
Flexible metal–organic frameworks (FMOFs) might be the good candidates to accommodate various guest molecules, as most of them features breathing effect. Inspired by this conjecture, Xiang and coworkers chose the flexible MOF (FJU-31) as host framework to load various $-\mathrm{OH}$ containing proton carriers, including $\operatorname{Ch}{}=$ cyclohexanol, ${\mathrm{Hq~}}=$ hydroquinone, and Bu $\c=$ butanol, affording three new FMOF-based proton conductors $(\mathrm{F})\mathrm{U}{-}31@\mathrm{G}$ , $\mathrm{~G~}=\mathrm{~Ch~}$ ,
Hq, and Bu).[45] As expected, the resulting flexible MOF (FJU$31@\mathrm{G})$ displayed large breathing effect with largest amplitudes of $65\%$ and a temperature-induced guest-related SC to SC structural transformation (Figure 13a). Thereinto, F $\operatorname{\lceilU-31@\mathrm{Hq}}$ containing hydroquinone molecules with a high melting point and small $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ value showed a high anhydrous proton conductivity of $2.65\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $125~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , low activation energy $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.18\ \mathrm{eV},$ and the widest working temperature range (from $-40$ to $125~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) for stable proton transport among the crystalline porous materials (Figure  13b,c). In the near future, the flexible CPMs can be regarded as a new type of host framework to encapsulate various proton carriers with numerous movable $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ , suitable for wide temperature fuel cell application.
During the preparation of this manuscript, Kitagawa and co-workers realized a hybrid proton conductor with high anhydrous proton conductivity of $4.4\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at room temperature and a low activation energy of $0.2{\mathrm{~eV}},$ which was prepared by impregnating ionic liquids $(\mathrm{EMI})[\mathrm{N}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}]$ into the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based metal–organic framework (PCN-777) with the largest pore cavities size of 35 Å.[137] Theoretical calculations indicated that the ionic liquids (ILs) within mesoporous materials preferentially formed an interface region $(\approx1\bar{0}\mathrm{~\AA~}$ thickness), meanwhile the diffusion rate of ions would be greatly limited. If the host frameworks possess a pore cavity large enough to afford the guest to stay away from this interface regions, the additional ionic liquids located at the core of the pores and will exhibit high ion diffusivity in the nanospace similar to the original bulk ILs (Figure 14a). As shown in Figure 14b, the ion diffusivity $(D_{298\mathrm{K}})$ of filling level $(62.5\%)$ hybrid sample was calculated to be $3.1\times$ $10^{-11}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , which was comparable to those of typical bulk ILs (1.6 to $9.9\times10^{-11}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ ). Notably, the hybrid material exhibited high proton conduction performances $\mathrm{(>10^{-2}~S~c m^{-1}}$ above 343 K) over a wide temperature range from 248 to $403\mathrm{~K~}$ and more importantly, the conductivity value surpassed that of the bulk ionic liquids at subzero temperatures $(<263~\mathrm{~K~},$ , Figure 14c).
## 3. Single-Crystal and Thin-Film Proton Conduction
So far, the measurement of AC impedance spectra of $\mathrm{CPs}/$ MOFs-based proton conductors has been carried out almost exclusively on the pelleted samples, and only a small number of reported MOFs were tested by using the single-crystal or nanofilm samples.[138–142] Although the measurement of pelleted samples is beneficial to provide macroscopic proton conducting properties of one material, it is difficult to reveal the inherent proton conduction mechanism. In order to investigate the anisotropic proton conduction behavior of CPMbased proton conductors, Kim and co-workers[143] realized the first example of porous organic molecule by employing the single-crystal sample in 2011. The isomorphous porous organic molecule $\mathrm{CB}[6]\cdot1.1\mathrm{HCl}\cdot11.3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and CB[6] $1.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\cdot6.4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ showed a similar honeycomb structure, contained 1D channel with the pore aperture size of $6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ along the crystallographic $\scriptstyle{c}$ axis, which were filled with water and acid molecules to form hydrogen-bonding networks. The single crystal conductivities of $\mathrm{CB}[6]{\cdot}1.1\mathrm{HCl}{\cdot}11.3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CB}[6]\cdot1.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}{\cdot}6.4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ along the channel $(c$ -axis) direction were found to be $2.4\times10^{-2}$ and $4.3\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, under $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $98\%$ RH, which were over 10 times higher than those of pelleted samples under similar conditions. In addition, the proton conductivities of single crystal sample perpendicular to the $\boldsymbol{c}$ axis were $7.1\times10^{-5}$ and $5.0\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ , these values were almost three or four orders of magnitude lower than those along the $c$ -axis. Nonetheless, it is reasonable due to the anisotropic channel features of these materials. The above contribution opened a new avenue to insight into the proton transport mechanism of CPM-based proton conductors, which was also favorable for developing new type of solid electrolytes for fuel cells.
The concept of intrinsic proton conduction in coordination polymers first proposed by Kitagawa and co-workers in 2012.[138] They reported a 2D coordination framework $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{TzH})_{2}]$ that formed a continuous hydrogenbonding network between the layers. To visually assess the intralayer to interlayer. To justify that the proton conductive pathway in CoLa-II was along the [100] direction, they further tested the proton conductivity by using the singlecrystal sample. It was found that the conductivity value reached to $3.05\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ ( $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH), which was almost 100 times high than that of corresponding pelleted sample under the same conditions.
At the same time, Zheng and coworkers[144] reported a lanthanide MOF, $\mathrm{[Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{CO}_{3})(\mathrm{ox})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ wherein the coordinated water molecules and the adjacent oxalate groups formed a 1D hydrogenbonding arrays along the $a$ -axis direction. In order to directly demonstrate that the proton conduction pathway along the hydrogenbonded arrays, they performed AC impedance experiments employing the single crystal sample. The conductivity was measured of $2.14\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under anhydrous condition, slightly higher than the value of the microcrystalline pelleted sample $(2.08\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ and almost 4400 times higher than the value $(4.86\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ 1 along the $\boldsymbol{c}$ -axis direction.
Although some scientists are trying to explore the proton transfer mechanism by using the single-crystal sample, it’s still very challenging to insight into the proton transfer mechanism in crystalline porous materials. Recently, Zang and co-workers reported a 2D Ln-MOF proton conductor, $\left(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ $\mathrm{[Eu(L)]}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}~=~5\$ -(phosphonomethyl)isophthalic acid),[145] which displayed well-defined 1D hydrogen-bonding arrays along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis between the counter cations $\mathrm{(Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{)^{+}}$ and phosphonate groups from an anionic framework (Figure  15a–c). Such hydrogeninherent proton conduction behavior of this compound, they conducted the AC impedance study by using the single crystal sample. The in-plane anhydrous conductivity was found to be $1.1\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which was close to the bulk conductivity of the pellet sample $(1.2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Moreover, the out-of-plane conductivity was measured to be $2.9\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which were almost two orders of magnitude lower than the in-plane one. Therefore, it can be sufficiently demonstrated that the intrinsic proton conduction along the direction parallel to the layers.
Subsequently, Kitagawa and co-workers[90] reported a 2D layered metal phosphonate framework CoLa-II in 2014, with the formula of $\mathrm{\small{[CoLa(notpH)(H_{2}O)_{6}]C l O_{4}}}\cdot\mathrm{{5H_{2}O}}$ . Interestingly, CoLa-II occurred solid-state phase transition above $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $93\%$ RH, formed a new phase CoLa-III, $\mathrm{[H}_{3}\mathrm{O}]$ $[\mathrm{CoLa}(\mathrm{notp})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]\mathrm{Clo}_{4}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Meanwhile, the conductivity of CoLa-III $(4.24\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ increased by an order of magnitude compared with pristine CoLa-II $(3.50\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ ), at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH, which was attributed to the phase transition process accompanied by the release of proton from bonding array endowed this sample with high single-crystal conductivity of $1.25\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis direction (Figure 15d). Meanwhile, there was no obvious proton conduction behavior observed along other crystal axis ( $\scriptstyle(a-$ or $b$ -axis) direction under the same conditions, which further indicated that protons were only transferred along the hydrogenbonding array direction. It was particularly noteworthy that this work had fully demonstrated the intuitive proton transport pathway in $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})[\mathrm{Eu(L)}]$ by combining a series of in situ variable-temperature characterization techniques, such as single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD), powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectrum (DRIFTS), and photoluminescence. Moreover, it can be observed that rare single crystal to single crystal (SC to SC) structural transformation between the isomorphic anionic framework and neutral framework was accompanied by the transfer of protons, by fine controlling the experimental process.
The study proton conduction of single crystal is critical to elaborate their proton transfer mechanisms and guide the
