## 4. MOF-Based Membranes for Fuel Cell
The development of proton conductive materials used as electrolytes in intermediate-temperature $(100-300\mathrm{~~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ PEMFC device remains a substantial challenge. Recently, Wang and co-workers realized a CP-based proton conductor, $\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{3}[Z r(H_{2/3}P O_{4})_{3}](Z r P),^{[149]}}$ in which the counter $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ cations located in a suitable position can be used not only as proton carriers, but also to connect the oxygen atoms from neighboring zirconium phosphate chains to form the infinite 1D hydrogenbonding pathway (Figure  20a,b). Therefore, the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZrP}}$ achieved a high anhydrous proton conductivity of $1.45\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which was comparable to the benchmark anhydrous proton conductor materials reported to date[53,124,136] (Figure 20c). In particularly, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZrP}}$ can be used directly as the electrolyte for intermediate-temperature PEMFC, affording a record-high peak power density of $12\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure  20d,e), which is over four times larger than those of representative CP-based PEMFCs.[150,151] Furthermore, they also employed the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZrP}}$ for the first time as solid-state electrolyte for assembling the direct methanol fuel cell (DMFC). This study shows a step forward toward the actual application of CP/MOF as solid electrolyte for fuel cells.
In 2013, Zhu and co-workers reported a chiral 2D Ca-MOF, $\left[[\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{Hpmpc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{HO}_{0.5}\right]_{n}.$ ,[152] was obtained from D-1- (phosphonomethyl) piperidine-3-carboxylic acid (D-Hpmpc) and CaO under solvothermal condition. Single structure revealed that the terminal coordinated water molecules and carboxylates in Ca-MOF can form continuous hydrogen-bonding chains, and the protonated tertiary amines can serve as proton carriers simultaneously (Figure  21a). The submicrorods sample of Ca-MOF exhibited a moderate proton conductivity of $6.9\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $298~\mathrm{K}_{:}$ under $53\%$ RH. To cope with the problem that MOF-based proton conductors can work under low humidity condition, they prepared a series of composite matrix membrane, termed as $\mathrm{Ca}$ -MOF-PVP-X. Amazingly, at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $53\%$ RH, the proton conductivity of Ca-MOF-PVP-50 reached up to $2.8\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , which was high than those of Ca-MOF $(6.9\times10^{-6}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ) and pristine PVP $(1.4\times10^{-8}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ under the identical conditions (Figure  21b–d). Lately, Zhu and co-workers further reported a novel hexaphosphate esterbased MOF, JUC-200,[153] prepared by phytic acid (PA) and zinc nitrate under solvothermal reaction (Figure 21g). JUC-200 exhibited excellent water/acid stability, and high proton conductivity of $1.62\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , under pure water circumstance. Furthermore, the hybrid membranes of $\mathrm{JUC-}200@\emptyset$ PVA-10 were fabricated and showed good proton conductivity of $1.25\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 21h). These two studies may be served as pioneer role for the actual application of MOF materials in PEMFCs.
Recently, Peng and co-workers realized a single-strand DNAthreaded hybrid proton exchange membrane DNA $@$ ZIF-8, which was constructed through a so-called solid confinement conversion strategy (Figure 22a).[154] Notably, the hybrid membrane of DNA $@$ ZIF-8-3/25 displayed a ultrahigh proton conductivity of $1.7\times10^{-1}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $97\%$ RH, since the adsorbed water molecules and DNA molecules with abundant phosphate and amidogen groups could form a continuous hydrogen-bonding networks within the pore cavities of ZIF-8 (Figure 22b,c). Similar to most hydrated proton conductors, the proton conductivity of DNA $@$ ZIF-8-3/25 was highly dependent on relative humidity. Furthermore, owing to the small pore entrance characteristics, the hybrid membrane DNA@ZIF8-3/25 displayed ultralow methanol permeability with the value of $1.25\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , remarkably lower than the value of $5.59\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for commercial Nafion 115 membrane. More importantly, the DNA $@$ ZIF-8-3/25-PVDF hybrid membrane is the first MOF-based proton exchange membrane used for DMFC, featuring a peak power density of $9.87\mathrm{~mW~cm}^{-2}$ (Figure 22d), its performance is comparable to those of covalent organic frameworks membranes used in $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ fuel cells.[155,156] The research will encourage extensive study on other CPMsbased hybrid membranes for fuel cells usage in the near future.
Since Zhu and co-workers[152] first time achieved that submicrorods MOF was incorporated into a PVP matrix to fabricate the polymer composite membranes for PEMs, hereafter, various types of proton conductive hybrid membranes with well-known MOFs (e.g., MIL-101,[157,158] UiO-66,[159,160] and $Z\mathrm{IF}{\cdot}8^{[154,161]})$ as a filler have been prepared. The widely studied MOF-801 featured superior stability and water-adsorption behavior, built from the $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(C O O)_{12}]}$ SBUs with linear fumarate linkers (Figure  23a). Recently, Ren and co-workers reported a polymer hybrid membrane $\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}801@\mathrm{PP}{\cdot}60,$ [162] which was fabricated from sub-micrometer-scale powers of MOF-801 and blending two component PVDF-PVP (PP, PVDF $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Poly(vinylidene fluoride)) matrix, and realized a high proton conductivity with the $\sigma$ value of $1.84\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $325\mathrm{~K~}$ and $98\%$ RH (Figure 23c). Moreover, this composite membrane was further used as a PEM for the practical $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ fuel cell test, exhibited the open-circuit voltage and maximum power density of $0.95\mathrm{~V~}$ and $2.2\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure 23d), respectively.
The concept of “acid–base pairs” in MOF-based proton conductor was first proposed by Ghosh and co-workers in 2014,[163] in which the conductors can work under anhydrous and humid conditions simultaneously. After that, Xiang and co-workers extended this concept (acid–base pair) to the PMO-based proton conductors,[136] which showed high proton conductivity working in the wide temperature window. To date, many MOF materials containing acid–base pairs have shown excellent proton conductivity.[30,126,163,164] Recently, Na and co-workers extended the concept of “acid–base pairs” to the preparation of polymer hybrid membrane.[157] First, they prepared a novel side-chain sulfonated polymer SNF-PAEK. Then, a series of amino-sulfonate-bifunctionalized MOF ( $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ termed as MNS) filled hybrid membranes were fabricated (Figure 24a,b). Among them, $\mathrm{MNS}@\mathrm{SNF}\mathrm{-PAEK}.3\%$ realized the conductivity value of $1.92\times10^{-1}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure $24\mathrm{c})$ , which is larger than those of pristine SNF-PAEK $(1.45\times10^{-1}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and recast Nafion $(1.34\times10^{-1}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ under similar condition.[165] More importantly, the hybrid membrane $(\mathrm{MNS}@\mathrm{SNF}.\mathrm{PAEK}.3\%)$ was further employed as PEMs to assembly the DMFC, afforded an excellent fuel cell performance with the maximum power density and open circuit voltages of $125.7\mathrm{~mw~cm}^{-2}$ and $0.839\mathrm{~V},$ respectively, significantly superior than the pristine membrane and Nafion (Figure 24d).
Up to now, MOF as filler embedded in polymer can effectively improve the proton conduction performances of composite membranes under humidity atmosphere; however, in anhydrous or over $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ condition, the situation is not satisfactory. In order to overcome this challenge, Zang and co-workers also adopted the “acid–base pairs” strategy to fabricate the dual MOF-cofilled hybrid membranes, in which UiO$66(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})$ and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ as filler embedded in chitosan (CS) matrix (Figure  25a).[159] The optimum dual MOF-cofilled hybrid membrane exhibited high proton conductivity with the value of $5.2\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ (at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH) and $3.78\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and anhydrous conditions), respectively. In contrast, each component of $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})$ , $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ , CS, and single MOF-filled hybrid membranes almost lost proton conduction behaviors under anhydrous conditions (Figure  25b). Furthermore, this dual MOF-cofilled hybrid membrane was also employed as PEMs to study the single $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ cell performance at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , acquired the value of $1.0\mathrm{V}$ and $10.6\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ for open circuit voltages and peak power density, respectively (Figure 25c).
## 5. Conclusions and Perspectives
Over the past decade, the versatile MOFs with well-defined crystal structure and rich structural tunability showed great potential and special advantages in proton conductor applications. Moreover, their crystalline nature may offer distinct opportunities to investigate the proton transport mechanism by employing the X-ray diffraction techniques, which can be served as an exciting guideline for further designing and synthesizing new type of MOF-based proton conductors. Herein, the surveyed samples have covered various effective strategies for achieving high proton conductivity in many promising MOFs. First, we chose several representative MOFs as platforms (e.g.,
MIL-101, UiO-66, HKUST-1, and $\beta$ -PCMOF-2), and elaborated that some universal or unique strategies can be used to improve their proton conductive performances. Then, we elaborated on the concept and importance of wide operating temperature range (especially for working under sub-zero temperature) for proton conductors, such materials displayed typical proton conductive behaviors under anhydrous atmosphere. In addition, the research progress in the exploration of proton transfer mechanism and anisotropic conduction behavior using MOF-based single crystal and thin film samples was also fully discussed. Finally, several promising MOF candidates were summarized as proton exchange membranes to assemble fuel cells.
Although MOF materials have made great progress in the application of proton conductors, even the conductivity values of some superior candidates $(>0.1\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , Table 1) are higher than the commercial Nafion materials $(>10^{-2}\ \mathrm{~S~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ , there are many remaining opportunities and challenges in this field. i) The water and thermal stability of such material has been greatly improved, however, their mechanical strength and processability have not received sufficient consideration. ii) Alkyl chain linear polymer functionalized organic ligands can be used to fabricate new type of poly-MOF-based proton conductor to ameliorate their mechanical properties and processability. iii) The linear polymers (e.g., polyaniline, poly(vinyl alcohol), and poly(acrylic acid)) were prepared through the in situ strategy within the MOF channel, which might not only provide a continuous pathway for proton conduction but also promote the assembly performance of the membrane electrode. iv) For some unique MOF samples, due to their structural disorder or defect, limited knowledge of the proton transfer mechanisms restricts their rational manufacturing. In situ experiments and/or advanced characterization techniques (e.g., quasi-elastic neutron scattering and solid-state NMR), and theoretical calculations would be combined to investigate the proton conduction mechanism. v) The incorporation of new types of proton carriers within the pore or the employment of external stimuli might be beneficial for improving the proton conduction performances of such materials.[123,166] On the other hand, the conductive MOFs with switchable behavior can be applied as chemical sensor to detect external stimuli, such as gas,[167,168] light irradiation, electric and magnetic field. vi) The latest study has shown that MOF with switched hydrogen bond pathway may simultaneously achieve resistive switching and rectifying effects,[169] such material can be used as resistive random-access memory (RRAM) for information storage. Meanwhile, it opens a novel direction for the potential applications of conductive MOF materials.
Overall, MOFs have been widely validated as promising solid electrolytes for proton exchange membrane fuel cells. For the practical applications, the prerequisite is to expand the production of MOFs in a convenient, low-cost, and environmentally friendly manner. It is foreseeable that worldwide efforts toward these challenges on this field will surely bring a bright future to fuel cell technology.
